  "query_preview": "..Today...Lingering cloud cover this morning has \ndelayed the initial start of convection this morning. However, \nanother round of storms looks to develop around 1 PM MST over the \nhigher terrain. Storms will then move southwest through the \nafternoon off the higher terrain, as development becomes outflow \ndriven. Thus, the greatest chances will largely be from Grand \nCanyon to Flagstaff to the White Mountains, and areas southwest. \nSome additional isolated activity looks possible along Black Mesa.\n\nInstability and shear, while still weak, look to be the greatest \nacross Yavapai and Gila counties. So a few strong to severe \nthunderstorms will be possible this afternoon in these areas. \nOtherwise, flash flooding will be the primary hazard. \n\nSaturday and Sunday...Isolated to scattered coverage remains into \nthe weekend as the high gradually begins to weaken and wobble around \nthe Four Corners. Moisture remains largely focused across western \nand central Arizona, thus the greatest coverage over the weekend \nwill largely be from the Mogollon Rim, south and west. A strong to \nsevere storm can not be totally ruled out, but flash flooding will \nremain the primary hazard into the weekend.\n\nMonday through Thursday...The high begins to weaken into the start \nof next week, and moves into the Four Corners region. Sub-tropical \nmoisture increases further across northern Arizona, resulting in near \ndaily chances for scattered to widespread showers and thunderstorms. \nGiven the increased moisture, flash flooding will be primary hazard \nfor the coming week. Daily coverage however remains uncertain, and \nwill largely be determined by any lingering debris clouds. \nOtherwise, cooler temperatures and light winds prevail into next \nweek.",
